  values ('ME', 'long_answer', 'Discuss the principles and applications of estrus synchronization as a cornerstone of modern livestock breeding programs. Explain the hormonal mechanisms involved in any one of the synchronization protocols. Furthermore, evaluate the economic benefits and potential challenges of implementing an estrus synchronization program on a commercial farm, considering both time-based and cost-based factors. (20 marks)', 'Estrus synchronization manipulates the estrous cycle with hormones so that a group of females comes into heat (or ovulates) at a planned time. It is used for timed artificial insemination with superior semen, embryo transfer, planned calving to match feed and markets, and to overcome poor heat detection.
Example - Ovsynch: day 0 GnRH causes ovulation or luteinisation of the dominant follicle and starts a new follicular wave; day 7 PGF2α lyses the corpus luteum, progesterone falls; day 9 a second GnRH triggers a synchronized LH surge and ovulation; timed AI 16-20 hours later. (Alternative: two PGF2α injections 11-14 days apart, or a progesterone-releasing device for 7-9 days.)
Benefits: less time on heat detection, more cows bred early, shorter calving interval, uniform calf batches, efficient use of AI technicians and labour.
Challenges: cost of hormones and extra handling; conception rates vary (poor body condition, anoestrus, stress); needs good records, skilled staff and nutrition; failure means costly repeats.', null, '[{"point": "Principle: control the cycle so a group comes into heat together; applications (AI, timed breeding, embryo transfer)", "marks": 5}, {"point": "One protocol''s hormonal mechanism, e.g. Ovsynch GnRH-PGF2α-GnRH or PGF2α double injection or progesterone device", "marks": 6}, {"point": "Economic benefits: planned calving, less heat detection, more AI use, uniform batches", "marks": 5}, {"point": "Challenges: drug and labour costs, nutrition/body condition needed, variable conception, skills", "marks": 4}]'::jsonb, (select id from public.subjects where slug = 'subject-specialisation'),
